## Correction 2026-08-21: the premise below is wrong

The section "What Zotero 10 does and does not give us" concludes that Zotero
exposes no in-text structure. **That is true of `.zotero-ft-cache` and false of
Zotero.** The investigation read one file and generalised from it.

Zotero ships a *second* extraction, `Zotero.SDT`, and it is in the installed
build here -- Zotero **10.0**, BuildID **20260817151751**:

    chrome/content/zotero/xpcom/sdt.js                      16 011 B
    resource/document-worker/structured-document-text.js    23 496 B

Its per-attachment `.zotero-sdt-cache` pack carries exactly what this ticket
assumed was unavailable: **`pageIndex` and `pageLabel`** (real labels -- `'ix'`,
`'15'` -- falling back to ordinal), reader-navigable **`position`**,
**`flowClass`** with running heads and page numbers marked as excluded flows,
**`outlinePath`**, and a **`reference` flag** for bibliography entries.

So the chunker question is not "structure is unavailable, therefore tune two
constants". It is "structure exists; can we reach it?"

**Three obstacles, and the first is decisive.** No pack has been generated on
this library: **zero `.zotero-sdt-cache` files across 13 631 attachments**.

Two readings of that were attempted before the right one, and both failed the
same way -- `BuildID=20260817151751` is when Zotero *compiled* the release, and
it was used as the date the author *installed* it. The install is
`/opt/zotero7/app/omni.ja`, **2026-08-21 14:26**.

| | | |
|---|---|---|
| `746J4UL3/.zotero-reader-state` | 2026-08-17 15:45 | before install |
| `TB28BNW2/.zotero-reader-state` | 2026-08-20 21:52 | before install |
| local install | **2026-08-21 14:26** | |
| 1 036 `.zotero-ft-cache` rewritten | 14:27 onward | bulk re-index |
| reader sessions since install | **0** | |

Counts recorded in `bench/results/0007-sdt-probe.txt`, with the commands that
produced them. **There is no positive control**: nothing here demonstrates the
probe would find a `.zotero-sdt-cache` if one existed, because none does. A
check whose all-clear cannot be distinguished from "I could not look" is not a
check, so treat the zero as provisional until the one-PDF experiment below
produces a pack the same probe then finds.

So **no PDF has been opened in the reader since the upgrade**, and the
confident conclusion drawn from the second reading -- that the reader path does
not invoke SDT -- is *unsupported*: that path has not run once. The 14:38
`.zotero-ft-cache` that looked like a reader session belongs to the bulk
re-index, consistent with the FTS5 rewrite in Zotero's release notes.

What the evidence does support is narrower: **the bulk full-text re-index
produced no SDT packs**, which rules out that path and only that path.

**The experiment was run 2026-08-21T20:16Z**, the timestamp its artifact carries. `Stadler2021` (`VH3PV9HD`) was opened in
Zotero, which reported `Indexed: yes`, and its `.zotero-ft-cache` was written at
22:13 -- after the 14:26 install, so extraction ran on the shipped 10.0 build.
**No `.zotero-sdt-cache` appeared, and the library-wide count is still zero.**

So: on a stock Zotero 10.0, full-text extraction runs and produces no SDT pack.
That is one path ruled out, and it is the path a user actually exercises.

**One residual, stated rather than glossed, because this ticket has already
been over-read twice.** No `.zotero-reader-state` was written for this item,
while the pre-install `TB28BNW2` has one -- so that file *is* produced when the
reader runs. Its absence leaves open whether the reader tab was opened or only
indexing was triggered from the item pane. Extraction demonstrably ran; whether
the *reader* path would produce a pack is narrowed, not settled.

Settling the residual costs one more minute: open the PDF in a reader **tab**,
close the tab, confirm `.zotero-reader-state` appears, and re-probe. Until
then, treat SDT as unreachable on a stock install for planning purposes, and
proceed on the configuration and measurement actions below.

Probe and its output: `bench/results/0007-sdt-probe.txt`.

Second, the pack is an **undocumented binary
format** (magic + `SDT_PACK_VERSION` + independently compressed block groups)
read through a bundled module; using it externally means porting that reader
from in-tree AGPL source. Third, it is **not exposed over the local API**, so an
HTTP client cannot ask for it at all -- only a process with filesystem access to
the Zotero data directory.

## Calibration available for free

zotero/zotero#6012 chunks for embedding with constants worth reading against
this ticket's, and they are measured rather than guessed:

| | zoteus today | Zotero #6012 |
|---|---|---|
| unit | characters | **model tokens**, counted with the model's own tokenizer |
| metadata / notes | 512 / 64 overlap | 120 min, **768 max**, 48 overlap |
| attachment text | 1200 / 150 | same budget, **section-aware** |
| structure | none | chunks respect section boundaries; each chunk's embed text is prefixed with its **outline path** ("Methods > Participants"), charged against the budget and dropped if it would exceed a quarter of it |

Two things to take from that even if SDT stays out of reach. A **token** budget
is the honest unit when the consumer is an embedder with a token limit -- 768 is
a ceiling there, not a target, so chunks come out paragraph-sized. And
**captions and image descriptions are always their own chunk** regardless of
size, which a fixed window cannot express.

## Revised actions

1. **Done 2026-08-22: no pack.** Extraction ran and produced none, so SDT is a
   *future* option rather than a present one, and actions 2-4 proceed without
   it. One residual check remains (open in a reader tab, confirm
   `.zotero-reader-state` appears, re-probe) to close the last path.
2. Config knobs as originally planned (`ZOTEUS_INDEX_CHUNK_SIZE` /
   `_OVERLAP` and the full-text pair), defaults unchanged.
3. Consider a token budget rather than a character budget, following #6012.
   This is not cosmetic: the consumer is an embedder with a hard token limit,
   and characters are a proxy for tokens that varies by language.
4. Measure at two or three geometries and record in `bench/`.

## Context

Tracker: 0001, but landing after it -- changing chunk geometry changes results,
which is exactly what "same corpus in, same results out" forbids while the
storage swap is being validated.

Three chunk geometries exist, none reachable from configuration:

| where | geometry | how set |
|---|---|---|
| metadata passages, `index-manager.ts:560` and `:875` | 512 / 64 overlap | bare `chunkText()` defaults |
| attachment full text, `index-manager.ts:890` | 1200 / 150 | `FULLTEXT_CHUNK_SIZE`, exported const |
| passage locators, `fulltext/passages.ts:72` | 800 / 100 | bare `chunkWithOffsets()` defaults |

`grep -n "chunk" src/config.ts` returns nothing. `chunkText` is a free
function called directly at three sites -- no interface, no injection point.

512 was tuned for a resident JS index, where every passage cost heap. On disk
that constraint is gone and larger chunks mean fewer rows and a smaller index,
but nobody can measure the tradeoff while the number is a default nobody can
reach.

Note the two geometries are not merely different, they disagree: a search hit
comes from `chunkText`, while the locator `zotero_get_fulltext` reports for it
comes from `chunkWithOffsets` at a different size and a different treatment of
whitespace.

## What Zotero 10 does and does not give us

Investigated on the author's own library (13 631 cached attachments,
`~/data/Zotero`), because the hunch was that Zotero extracts structure worth
chunking on.

**It is smart about extraction, and does not expose structure.**

- `.zotero-ft-info` is a `pdfinfo` dump -- `Pages`, `Title`, `Author`,
  `Subject`, `Keywords`, page size, PDF version. Present in 200 of 200 files
  sampled. The API surfaces the useful part of it: `/items/KEY/fulltext`
  returns `indexedPages` and `totalPages`, which `get-fulltext.ts:111` already
  reads. pdfjs re-extraction is the fallback for *unindexed* attachments only,
  not a duplicated page count -- that part of the design is sound.
- `.zotero-ft-cache` is a **flat text stream with line breaks and no page
  markers**. Form feeds appear in **5 of 300** caches sampled (1,7%). So there
  are no page boundaries to chunk on, and none of Zotero's own page knowledge
  reaches the text.
- No headings, no sections, no paragraph semantics beyond newlines. What
  zoteus displays as structure is invented downstream: `findSection` is a
  regex guess over preceding lines, and `approxPage` is linear interpolation
  (`ceil((charStart+1)/totalChars * totalPages)`) -- a proportional estimate
  presented in the same field as a real page number.

So chunking on document structure is not available from Zotero today. The
useful geometry question is the boring one: what size and overlap, measured.

## A second finding, recorded here rather than as its own ticket

**Zotero 10 already runs FTS5**, and its tokenizer choices are worth reading
against 0002's:

    fulltextContent     fts5(text, tokenize='unicode61', content='', contentless_delete=1)
    fulltextContentCJK  fts5(text, tokenize='ascii',     content='', contentless_delete=1)
    fulltextNotes       fts5(text, tokenize='trigram',   content='', contentless_delete=1)

Two consequences.

`content=''` makes those tables **contentless**: they can be MATCHed but the
text cannot be read back. That is why zoteus reads the cache rather than
querying Zotero's index, and it forecloses the obvious "just reuse Zotero's
FTS5" shortcut -- worth stating in the #10 writeup before someone proposes it.

Zotero maintains a **separate CJK table** because `unicode61` does not segment
CJK; it falls back to `ascii` there. 0002 chose
`unicode61 remove_diacritics 2` and inherits the same blind spot: a Chinese or
Japanese document indexes as very few enormous tokens. Below the severity floor
for this library (French and English), so it is recorded, not ticketed -- but
it is a real limitation of the tokenizer decision and should be named rather
than discovered.

## Actions

1. `ZOTEUS_INDEX_CHUNK_SIZE` / `ZOTEUS_INDEX_CHUNK_OVERLAP` and the full-text
   pair in `config.ts`, defaulting to today's exact values (512/64, 1200/150)
   so nothing changes by default.
2. Thread them from config to the three call sites. No strategy interface:
   a pluggable chunker is speculative until a second strategy exists, and
   YAGNI applies.
3. Decide deliberately whether `chunkWithOffsets` (800/100) follows the same
   settings or keeps its own -- it serves locators, not retrieval, and the two
   jobs may legitimately want different windows. Say which, and why, in the code.
4. Re-run `bench/fts5_bench.mjs` at two or three geometries and record index
   size, build time and query latency against passage count.

## Test

Red step: assert the default configuration reproduces today's passage count on
a fixture exactly. That is the whole safety property -- a config knob whose
default silently changes the index is worse than a constant.

## Exit criteria

- [x] SDT reachability determined and recorded. **The answer changed on
      2026-08-22 when the residual was finally run -- see the correction at the
