      not. Artifact: `bench/results/0007-sdt-probe.txt`, fourth probe.
- [x] Chunk size and overlap reachable from configuration, defaults unchanged:
      `ZOTEUS_CHUNK_SIZE`, `ZOTEUS_CHUNK_OVERLAP`, `ZOTEUS_FULLTEXT_CHUNK_SIZE`,
      `ZOTEUS_FULLTEXT_CHUNK_OVERLAP`
- [x] Default-path passage count byte-identical to today on a fixture --
      `tests/features/search-chunk-geometry.test.ts`, which pins the defaults
      against their literal values rather than against themselves, and pins that
      a different geometry really does change the passages so the knob is not
      decorative
- [x] `STATE.md`'s measurement table distinguishes the corpora -- superseded
      2026-08-22 by a single-corpus comparison: both backends over the same
      360 811 passages, no columns that are not comparable
- [x] Size/overlap tradeoff measured and recorded: superseded, and deliberately.
      The tradeoff cannot be measured honestly while the geometry is unstamped,
      because an index built at one geometry and served at another is a silent
      mixture. Stamping it beside the watermark (this ticket) is the
      prerequisite; the sweep itself buys nothing the chantier needs, since the
      shipped geometry is not changing. Recorded as not done, with the reason.

## Closing note, 2026-08-22

Closed with one residual that is not this repo's to close: whether Zotero's
reader tab writes an SDT pack. Everything reachable from a local API has been
probed. If a future session wants it, the whole experiment is: open one PDF in
Zotero's reader, then re-run the probe in `bench/results/0007-sdt-probe.txt`.

## Correction, 2026-08-22: the reader path produces packs after all

This ticket closed with one residual it could not settle from here -- whether
Zotero's *reader* writes a `.zotero-sdt-cache` pack, where bulk extraction
demonstrably does not. The author opened two PDFs. It does.

    2026-08-22 08:53   140 215 B   GMAP993G/.zotero-sdt-cache
    2026-08-22 08:54   345 539 B   BSMCQ6BM/.zotero-sdt-cache

Library-wide count 0 -> 2, and the two are exactly the two items opened.

**The positive control fires, which is what makes this different from the three
probes before it.** Each of those reported zero and could not show it would have
seen a pack if one existed -- an all-clear indistinguishable from "I could not
look", flagged in the artifact at the time. Here `.zotero-reader-state` appears
for both items, so the probe demonstrably sees the reader path, and the packs
appear beside it.

**What the ticket recorded was therefore wrong in its implication.** "Extraction
runs, SDT does not, so structure-aware chunking is a future option" was accurate
about extraction and read naturally as a verdict on SDT. It was not: SDT runs, on
a different trigger, and the wording did not distinguish "this path does not
invoke it" from "nothing does". Quoted rather than deleted, the way 0009's
retraction is handled here.

**What it does and does not open.** Packs are created lazily, per reading
session, for what a human opens: **2 of 13 631 attachments with extracted text**.
So SDT cannot be a general chunking input -- there is no bulk operation this
project can invoke to produce them, and the coverage of a research library will
always be the subset someone has read. What it could support is *enrichment* of
exactly those items: real page labels and outline paths for the papers a user
actually works with, on top of the proportional page estimate the index carries
today.

**And reaching the content is its own problem.** The pack is not JSON. It is a
custom binary container -- magic `89 53 44 54 0d 0a 1a 0a`, a u32 offset table,
payload sections that are not raw deflate at those offsets and hold no printable
text. Zotero ships the decoder (`resource/document-worker/structured-document-text.js`);
we would be reimplementing or vendoring it. Not attempted, and not costed.

No ticket filed. This is an opportunity rather than a defect, nothing is broken,
and the severity floor says report it here rather than mint work nobody has
asked for.
